vi: skip the raw-dump teardown when streaming never started

When enabling a VI channel fails, the release that follows runs
vi_stop_streaming(), and that tears down the raw-dump queue. The queue is set
up only at the end of vi_start_streaming(), so the teardown walks a zeroed
list head and the kernel oopses in isp_buf_remove() with interrupts off.

Return early when the queue was never set up.

---
--- a/interdrv/vi/chip/cv181x/vi_raw_dump.c
+++ b/interdrv/vi/chip/cv181x/vi_raw_dump.c
@@ -450,6 +450,13 @@
 }
 void isp_raw_dump_deinit(void)
 {
+	/*
+	 * vi_stop_streaming() also runs when streaming never started, as on the
+	 * release after a failed channel enable, and the queue is only set up at
+	 * the end of vi_start_streaming().
+	 */
+	if (!g_raw_dump_work.raw_dump_vb_q.rdy_queue.next)
+		return;
 	raw_dump_wq_handler(&g_raw_dump_work.worker);
 	cancel_work_sync(&g_raw_dump_work.worker);
 }
